  <blockquote class="ccarp-opening-quote">
    <p>“Computation is the medium of our lifetimes—it’s not just a technical medium but a cultural medium as well. We should explore the culture of computation.”</p>
    <cite>Myron Krueger</cite>
  </blockquote>

  <section class="ccarp-introduction">
    <h2>Catalog description</h2>
    <p>Introduction to creative coding as a method for research, discovery, and artistic inquiry. Using computational tools the work calls for, students will tackle a question of their own that will be the base for work that moves between technical rigor and public, cultural, or infrastructural critique, built with attention to legibility, delight, and narrative voice. Students will explore the history of computational thinking and creation tools, from Turing machines to early interactive systems to contemporary live coding practices. The study culminates in a final project, with an emphasis on making and documentation throughout.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="ccarp-section-heading">
      <h2>Phase structure</h2>
      <p>Semester-length, self-paced</p>
    </div>
    <p class="ccarp-section-intro">Rather than mapping to fixed weeks, structure the term as five phases. A student who has experience in the field might move through these quickly; a student who is newer might only make it to the first two. Remember, <a href="https://www.cmu.edu/news/stories/archives/2023/march/the-myth-of-the-fast-learner">there is no such thing as a fast learner</a>, only those with more experience and with less experience.</p>

    <ol class="ccarp-phases">
      <li>
        <div class="ccarp-phase-number">01</div>
        <div>
          <h3>Orientation &amp; Historical Grounding</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Research problem space; bring in multiple inspirations.</li>
            <li><strong>Deliverable:</strong> a one-paragraph research question.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="ccarp-phase-number">02</div>
        <div>
          <h3>Build</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Set goals for the project and set up a project management system.</li>
            <li>Choose specific goals, like a venue, a conference note, or an online mention, so the research has a life after the semester ends.</li>
            <li><strong>Deliverable:</strong> a rough, ugly, working prototype that tests one core mechanic of the idea.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="ccarp-phase-number">03</div>
        <div>
          <h3>Deepen</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Deliverable:</strong> a revised prototype plus a short written reflection on what the critique changed.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="ccarp-phase-number">04</div>
        <div>
          <h3>Outside Critique</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>An outside researcher will look at the question and progress and offer a critique.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="ccarp-phase-number">05</div>
        <div>
          <h3>Documentation &amp; Presentation</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Make the research legible.</li>
            <li><strong>Written documentation:</strong> what was the question, what did you build, what surprised you, what would you change.</li>
            <li>Public-facing presentation (post or show or performance) of the finished project.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <div class="ccarp-two-column">
    <section>
      <h2>Weekly rhythm</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Check-in:</strong> show what’s running, not what’s planned. If nothing runs, talk about why. There will be an emphasis on having a regular practice of “computational sketching” to regularly make small projects and iterate off them.</li>
        <li>Bring one weekly reference (paper, tool, or code). Keep historical grounding active through every phase.</li>
        <li>Keep a running log (even informal: a markdown file, an Are.na board, whatever is maintainable) of what was tried and discarded. This becomes the spine of the final documentation.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Deliverables + grading</h2>
      <dl class="ccarp-grading">
        <div><dt>Research question</dt><dd>15%</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Comparative survey of inspirations (and anti-inspirations)</dt><dd>15%</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Working foundational prototype</dt><dd>15%</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Midpoint critique + revised prototype</dt><dd>15%</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Final prototype</dt><dd>20%</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Written documentation</dt><dd>20%</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Live demonstration / performance</dt><dd>where applicable</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>
  </div>

