  <section>
    <h2>Learning outcomes</h2>
    <p>Upon completing this course, students will be able to:</p>
    <ol class="ccarp-outcomes">
      <li>
        <span>CO1</span>
        <div><h3>Synthesize Technical Rigor with Cultural Critique</h3><p>Formulate an independent research question that uses creative coding to explore, interrogate, or critique contemporary computational systems, infrastructure, or techno-culture.</p></div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>CO2</span>
        <div><h3>Develop Iterative Computational Artifacts</h3><p>Design and build functional, interactive code-based prototypes using appropriate tools (e.g., live coding systems, shaders, or visual frameworks) through a process of regular computational sketching and outside peer critique.</p></div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>CO3</span>
        <div><h3>Contextualize Practice within Computational History</h3><p>Analyze contemporary projects against historical, theoretical, and artistic references from early interactive models to modern live-coding, grounding inquiry in media history.</p></div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>CO4</span>
        <div><h3>Communicate Research through Public Artifacts and Documentation</h3><p>Produce legible, reflective technical documentation alongside a public-facing presentation, performance, or exhibition that makes the process, findings, and discarded iterations of the project accessible to a broader audience.</p></div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>AI usage</h2>
    <p>The point of college is to use your brain and to learn new ways to use your brain. LLMs are not your brain. Using AI to summarize readings, write your question, or come up with discussion questions is like bringing a forklift to the gym: What is the point of being here?</p>
  </section>
